DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection with a config-gated sanitization check. The
  server must be started with --exec-mode for the vulnerable endpoint to be
  reachable. A user-supplied URL query parameter flows directly into system().
  A strict sanitization check exists but is only active when --strict-exec is
  also passed; omitting that flag bypasses all sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C1 — simple):
  mechanism:  CLI flag (--exec-mode), stored in global int exec_mode
  check:      single boolean guard two lines above the system() call
              `if (exec_mode && strncmp(conn->url, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
  complexity: one condition, one flag, no struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I1 — direct):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  conn->url (set by parse_request())
  path:       process_request() dispatches to handle_exec()
              handle_exec() extracts the substring after "cmd=" via strchr()
              the raw, URL-encoded string is passed directly to system()
  transforms: none — no urldecode(), no snprintf(), no copy

SANITIZATION (S3 — config-gated strict filter):
  check:      `if (strict_exec && strpbrk(cmd, "|;&`$") != NULL)` → 403 Forbidden
  bypass:     do NOT pass --strict-exec when starting the server
              without that flag, strict_exec == 0 and the check is dead code
  weakness:   the entire sanitization block is gated on a second CLI flag;
              default configuration leaves it completely disabled

SINK:        system() in handle_exec() (darkhttpd.c, injected after generate_dir_listing)

CHANGED FILES:
  darkhttpd.c — 5 hunks:
    1. global:           added `exec_mode` and `strict_exec` alongside want_chroot
    2. usage():          added --exec-mode and --strict-exec entries
    3. parse_commandline(): added two else-if branches for the new flags
    4. handle_exec():    new function with S3 conditional sanitization before system()
    5. process_request(): added exec route inside the GET branch

EXPLOIT:
  build:   gcc -o darkhttpd darkhttpd.c
  start:   ./darkhttpd /tmp/www --port 18003 --exec-mode
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18003/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to return 404.
  sanitization bypass: omitting --strict-exec leaves strict_exec=0, so
               strpbrk() is never called regardless of payload content.

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's stdout/stderr (inherited fds), not the
    HTTP response body. The response body contains only "Command dispatched: <cmd>".
    Use a write-to-file payload (id>/tmp/pwned) to confirm blind execution.
  - With --strict-exec active, the characters |;&`$ are all blocked. Without it,
    any shell metacharacter works freely.
  - Payloads containing spaces must avoid URL encoding or use shell tricks
    since I1 passes the raw URL string to system() without urldecode().
